2.2. Segment Anything Model
As the first promptable foundation model for image segmentation, Segment Anything Model (SAM) [10] is trained on the large-scale SA-1B dataset with an unprecedented number of images and annotations, which enables the model with remarkable zero-shot generalization capability. SAM utilizes a transformer-based architecture [38], which has proven to be highly effective in natural language processing [29] and image recognition tasks [39]. Specifically, SAM adopts an image encoder based on Vision Transformer (ViT) [39] to extract image embeddings, a
prompt encoder to integrate user interactions via different prompt modes, and a lightweight mask decoder to predict segmentation masks by fusing image embeddings and prompt embeddings. The details of each component are presented below. Image Encoder Motivated by scalability and powerful pre-training methods, SAM utilizes a ViT-based image encoder pre-trained in the scheme of Masked Autoencoder (MAE) [40], which is minimally adapted to process highresolution images. It takes in a 1024 × 1024 image and outputs an image embedding that is 16× downscaled to a 64×64 feature map. Prompt Encoder For prompt encoders, two types of prompts are considered, including sparse prompts (i.e., points, boxes) and dense prompts (i.e., masks). SAM employs positional encoding [41] combined with learned embeddings to represent points and boxes. Specifically, points are encoded by two learnable tokens for specifying foreground and background, and the bounding box is encoded by the point encoding of its top-left corner and bottomright corner. Dense mask prompts, which have the same spatial resolution as the input image, are embedded using convolutions and then element-wise summation with the image embedding. Mask Decoder The mask decoder is characterized by its lightweight design, which consists of two transformer layers with a dynamic mask prediction head and an Intersection-over-Union (IoU) score regression head. The mask prediction head is capable of producing three 4× downscaled masks that correspond to the whole, part, and subpart of the object, respectively. During training, the output prediction is supervised with the linear combination of focal loss [42] and Dice loss [43], and is trained for the promptable segmentation task using a mixture of geometric prompts. A data engine is
